                    rec_none:               `No recognition scheme this round (€0)`,

                    r4_commit_label:        `The strike ballot is 45 days out. What commitment does the CEO take into the works council?`,
                    r4_seq_label:           `How do you sequence identification and the EBIT-to-8% mandate for the board?`,
                    r4_conv_label:          `Conviction: how firmly do you defend that symbols and signals — not pay alone — drive this? (%)`,
                    commit_credible:        `A specific, keepable commitment — the trigger-based profit-share and a co-decided no-surprise-layoffs process`,
                    commit_overpromise:     `Promise everything to kill the vote — full profit-share, no restructuring ever, and a 5% raise`,
                    commit_stonewall:       `Concede nothing — refuse to negotiate and dare them to strike`,
                    seq_idfirst:            `Identification first — rebuild belonging, let retention, effort and adoption follow, hold the board to the order`,
                    seq_both6:              `Promise the board both — identification at 60 AND EBIT at 8% by month six`,
                    seq_ebitfirst:          `EBIT first — chase the 8% now with more cuts; engagement can come later`,

                    warn_candor_range:      `Candor must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_sup_range:         `Supervisor investment must be between €0 and €1.5M (0–1500 in €000s).`,
                    warn_conv_range:        `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_both:          `<strong>Good — you're treating both halves.</strong> People here neither feel proud anymore nor can say what the firm stands for under PE ownership. Bergami's point is that you must feed cognitive AND affective identification; starve either and belonging stays broken.`,
                    coach_r1_onesided:      `<strong>You've reached for only one half of identification.</strong> Pour everything into emotion and workers still can't say what this company stands for; feed only the cognitive story and they understand the strategy but feel nothing. Bergami splits it deliberately — fix both.`,
                    coach_r1_denial:        `<strong>“It will recover on its own” is how you stay at 31.</strong> A 43-point fall from the 2021 level of 74 is not a normal post-sale dip. Something structural broke — name it, or you'll preside over the slide to a third CEO.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named both halves of identification AND located the root in a breached psychological contract — the frozen profit-share, the cold layoffs, the extraction story. That is exactly the lens that lets your later moves repair the right thing.`,
                    coach_r1_market:        `<strong>The market explains anxiety, not a 43-point collapse in belonging.</strong> Demand softened everywhere, yet most peers aren't at 31. The cause is closer to home: a contract this workforce feels was broken. Diagnose that.`,
                    coach_r1_workers:       `<strong>Careful — blaming an “entitled old guard” is the comfortable answer.</strong> These are the people who called it “la famiglia” with pride at 74. They didn't change; what was done to them did. Diagnose the breach, not the workforce.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>Pretending the rupture didn't happen reads as evasion.</strong> Every employee lost a colleague or a promise. Low candor from the new CEO says “they're hiding it too,” and credibility — the thing you most need — falls further.`,
                    coach_r2_honest:        `<strong>An honest heritage narrative does the work.</strong> Naming the breach and honouring “la famiglia” while charting a believable path is sensegiving and contract repair at once — it earns the credibility every later move depends on.`,
                    coach_r2_spin:          `<strong>“An exciting new chapter” is spin, and they can smell it.</strong> Saying nothing about the layoffs or the frozen scheme while sounding upbeat reads as the next slick owner. You can't rebuild a shared identity by pretending the rupture didn't happen.`,
                    coach_r2_silence:       `<strong>Silence from the new CEO reads as guilt.</strong> Every person in that room lost a colleague or a promise; “looking forward only” tells them you're hiding it too. Acknowledge the breach without re-opening the wound.`,
                    coach_r2_tour:          `<strong>An honest narrative carried in person lands.</strong> A credible listening tour of both plants — on the shop floor, by name — turns words into a deed they can see. That consistency between saying and showing up is what a cynical workforce is testing for.`,
                    coach_r2_memo:          `<strong>A video memo is not showing up.</strong> Identification is rebuilt in presence, not broadcast. A workforce primed for cynicism reads a polished intranet post as exactly the distance they expect from PE owners.`,
                    coach_r2_fiat:          `<strong>You renamed it “Officina Nostra” and announced it Monday.</strong> But you designed it in the boardroom and handed it down — exactly like the owners did. The name is “ours”; the process is still “theirs,” and adoption won't move from 22% because the symbol contradicts the experience.`,
                    coach_r2_mandate:       `<strong>Mandate-and-audit buys charting, not ownership.</strong> Ordering the lean program harder is what made it “the owners' project.” Co-ownership, not compliance, is what converts 22% into something real.`,
                    coach_r3_trigger:       `<strong>A trigger-based reinstatement repairs the contract honestly.</strong> Tying the profit-share to a credible, shared turnaround signal says “we rise together” without making a promise you might break — and it's the breach they care about most.`,
                    coach_r3_money:         `<strong>You reached for the wallet first.</strong> Identification was 74 in 2021 on a SMALLER bonus than today. A worker told twice that “la famiglia” is gone won't feel part of it because you wired €800. Pay buys attendance, not belonging — what symbolic act tells them this company is theirs again?`,
                    coach_r3_frozen:        `<strong>Leaving the scheme frozen and “talking later” keeps the wound open.</strong> The frozen profit-share is the central broken promise. Acknowledging and repairing it — even partially, on a trigger — is the price of credibility you can't defer.`,
                    coach_r3_nosup:         `<strong>You've under-invested in supervisors.</strong> The front-line supervisor is where “the company” is felt every day. Leave them unequipped and your fine narrative never reaches the shop floor; they need to become carriers of the identity, not just enforcers.`,
                    coach_r4_credible:      `<strong>A specific, keepable commitment is how you defuse the ballot honestly.</strong> The trigger-based profit-share plus a co-decided no-surprise-layoffs process is something you can actually keep — peace bought with a promise you'll honour, not the next breach.`,
                    coach_r4_stonewall:     `<strong>Conceding nothing dares them to strike.</strong> A walkout at peak OEM demand costs ~€1.2M/week and ruptures customer contracts — and it confirms to the workforce that the new CEO is just the fund's enforcer. Find the keepable commitment.`,
                    coach_r4_idfirst:       `<strong>You held the line on the sequence.</strong> Identification rebuilds first and drives retention, discretionary effort and adoption after — and you committed to defend that order against the board's six-month EBIT impatience. That's the causal logic of the whole turnaround.`,
                    coach_r4_both6:         `<strong>You promised the board identification at 60 AND EBIT at 8% by month six.</strong> Belonging rebuilds slowly, then drives performance — not the reverse. Promise both on the same clock and you'll chase EBIT with cuts, bury identification, and lose both.`,
                    coach_r4_ebitfirst:     `<strong>Chasing EBIT first with more cuts buries identification.</strong> This is the fund's old playbook — and the workforce knows it. Cut now and you push belonging, and the firm, past the point of recovery. The sequence is identification first.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>You promised full profit-share AND no restructuring ever AND a 5% raise.</strong> The vote collapses — for now. But the EBIT mandate hasn't changed, and in eight months you break one of those. That's the THIRD broken promise this workforce has heard. Identification doesn't dip then; it dies. What could you commit to and actually keep?`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed both halves and the breached contract.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-diagnosed the gap or its root cause.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Honest narrative, in person, change co-owned.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Spin/silence or a rename by fiat.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Trigger profit-share, craft recognition, supervisors.`,
                    sum_r3_money:           `Led with money — bought attendance, not belonging.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Reward signals under-built for identification.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Keepable commitment, identification-first sequence.`,
                    sum_r4_overpromise:     `Over-promised — bought a hollow, breakable peace.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Weak commitment or the wrong sequence.`,

                    insight_restored:       `“La famiglia” restored: you rebuilt identification past the ≥60 target with both halves moving together and a workforce that now believes you. You treated this as an identity problem, not a pay problem — and the discretionary effort, retention and change adoption the board wants will follow.`,
                    insight_onesided:       `You cleared the target but fed only one half of identification — cognitive and affective drifted apart. People either feel proud without knowing what the firm stands for, or understand the strategy without feeling part of it. Bergami's integration means both must move together to last.`,
                    insight_thin:           `You reached ≥60, but credibility stayed thin — the number is real on paper while the cynicism underneath persists. Without belief from a workforce primed to detect insincerity, the recovery is fragile.`,
                    insight_short:          `Identification fell short of the ≥60 target. The fund's patience is finite, and the playbook from here is a third CEO and deeper cuts. Re-examine which breach you left unacknowledged and whether you led with money instead of meaning.`,
                    insight_overspent:      `You over-spent the €6M envelope. The CEO's scarce resource is meaning, not money — and blowing the budget means the board claws it back and every gesture lands thinner. Restraint was part of the test.`,
                    insight_lowcred:        `Credibility stayed low: a workforce told twice that “la famiglia” is gone read your gestures as insincere. Identification can't rebuild on signals people don't believe.`,
                    insight_peak:           `Remarkable — you pushed identification back toward the firm's 2021 peak of 74. Sustained, this is the belonging that drove the company's best years.`,
                    insight_sequence:       `You held the identification-first sequence: belonging rebuilt, and retention, discretionary effort and change adoption now have a foundation to follow. That is the causal order the board needed to be held to.`,

                    hint_overspent:         `You've over-committed the €6M envelope — the board will claw it back and your gestures land thinner. Meaning, not money, is the scarce resource here.`,
                    hint_onesided:          `Cognitive and affective identification are drifting apart — you're feeding one half and starving the other. Bergami's integration means both must move together.`,
                    hint_cred:              `Credibility is dangerously low — a cynical workforce isn't believing your gestures. Acknowledge the breach honestly and show up in person before any reward signal will land.`,
                    hint_behind:            `Identification is lagging with little time left. Concentrate on the breached contract and on symbols a cynical 30-year forger would actually believe.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — identification and credibility both healthy. Now defend the identification-first sequence so retention and performance follow.`,

